WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.320
&nbsp;

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:07.030
Drama! Intrigue! False identities and whole
new personas! Sounds like the plot of a real

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:13.849
pot-boiler suspense novel. Or, idk, an episode
of WandaVision. But those are just some of

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:20.620
the reasons why real authors take on the hallowed
nom de plume, also known as the pen name!

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:27.440
To some people, the idea of a pen name seems
kind of weird. If I, a writer, am going to

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.300
put countless hours of hard work and thought
into my modern masterpiece, Hype House Blood

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.789
Bath, and I manage to catch the eye of an
agent or publisher who likes it—why wouldn’t

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:45.640
I want to put my own name underneath the title?
Why wouldn’t I want the world to know it

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:52.620
was me, Jimmy Squibbles, who toiled long and
hard on a 80,000 word epic about the pain

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:59.840
and suffering of being a Pocky influencer?
I have nothing to hide!

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:08.200
But from Stephen King to Ben Franklin to …., who
had their own secret aliases, to Mark Twain

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.970
and Dr. Seuss, whose aliases became so famous
that they are remembered by their pen names

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:20.470
and not their actual names, the nom de plume
has a long and proud history in the literary

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.470
world.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:27.560
Think of them like the original YouTubers—some
want to go by their real names, and some want

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:38.140
to create an online identity that connotes
less of a person and more of a brand. ….&nbsp;

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:44.480
While works written under anonymity or published
under a pseudonym might feel like an anomaly,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.240
much of the history of literature itself owes
itself to works without attachment to real

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:54.790
names. In his book Anonymity: A Secret History
of English Literature, author and professor

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.180
of English John Mullan muses that:

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.250
A good proportion of what is now English Literature
consists of works first published… without

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:06.570
their authors’ names. These works are now
collected in bookshops or libraries under

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:11.110
the names of those who wrote them, but the
processes by which they were attributed to

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:15.440
their authors are largely forgotten. It is
strange to think of… Pride and Prejudice

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.830
or Frankenstein being read without knowing
the identities of their creators, but so they

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.180
once were…

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.730
Mullan goes on to explain that this fascination
with pen names reached a zenith in the mid-19th

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:32.620
century, with scholars going so far as to
compile every piece of anonymously published

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:37.910
writing they could get their hands on, with
Scottish librarian Samuel Halkett compiling

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:45.930
anonymous material for a sort of dictionary
called the Dictionary of Anonymous and Pseudonymous

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:51.670
Literature of Great Britain. It clocked at
a massive nine volumes, all of which were

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:58.540
exclusively originally anonymous works. And
says Mullan, since its first print publication,

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.680
every well known-English author has been tied
to an entry within its pages.&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.750
So why would someone take a pen name, then,
since it seems to have been a trend as ubiquitous

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:14.230
as YA books titled (noun) of (nonu) and (noun).

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:20.170
Well, there are lots reasons. So, let’s
examine some notable examples of noms de plume

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.650
and why the real people they were attached
to were drawn to using them.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:30.100
Sometimes you have easy examples—Joseph
Conrad, who wrote Heart of Darkness (terrible

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:38.170
book), was originally named Jozef Konrad Korzeniowski
— and when he began publishing his works,

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:44.290
which were written in English also coincided
with him becoming a British subject, he anglicized

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:50.920
his name to reflect that. Much to the chagrin
of other Polish thinkers, who felt it disrespectful

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.670
to his country of birth.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:58.230
Some authors take names to hide past dramas
that actually happened to them in real life—like

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:03.490
author O. Henry, whose short stories often
concerned the minor tribulations of everyday

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:09.230
people living in New York City. Behind the
small, romanticized&nbsp; misunderstandings of

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:15.680
his fictional worlds was a man named William
Sydney Porter. Porter already had an established

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.769
writing career as a humorist, but after being
arrested and jailed for embezzlement, picked

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:28.710
up the pen again while in prison as O. Henry
in a bid to conceal that he was publishing

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:32.330
stories while incarcerated.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:36.760
Then you have writers like Theodor Geisel,
who while he had several pen names he worked

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:43.060
under, is most universally adored as children’s
author Dr. Seuss. Geisel took the name from

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:48.520
his mother, born Henrietta Seuss, while he
was an undergraduate at Dartmouth College—and,

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.420
despite how we pronounce it now, originally
pronounced it “SOICE”. He changed it to

00:04:53.420 --> 00:05:00.070
“SOOS” when he realized, in his own words,
that the pronunciation “evoked a figure

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:06.200
advantageous for an author of children’s
books to be associated with—Mother Goose.”

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:11.830
And then, just for funsies, added the Dr.
to his name as a joke…. dad had wanted him

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:16.220
to become a doctor. Sorry, dad.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:21.030
Some of the largest figures in modern popular
literature took pen names to differentiate

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:26.400
new works that didn’t fit the tone of what
they were most famous for—among these authors

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:33.390
are mystery writer Agatha Christie, who published
romance novels as Mary Westmacott, JK Rowling,

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:39.340
who ….. Sigh, … published her mystery-thriller
Cormoran Strike novels under the name Robert

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:46.980
Galbraith, and, perhaps most amusingly, Stephen
King, Our Boy™, who posted several works

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.340
as Richard Bachman.&nbsp;

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:54.170
At the beginning of King’s career, it had
been something of a truism amongst publishers

00:05:54.170 --> 00:06:00.230
that an author publishing more than one novel
per year would be distasteful to an audience.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:07.000
King, who like a shark that will die if it
stops swimming, will die if he stops writing

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:15.770
EVEN IF HE’S IN THE HOSPITAL CONVALESCING
FROM A NEAR FATAL CAR ACCIDENT jesus -- convinced

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:22.300
his publisher to let him publish several titles
as Richard Bachman. And when the ruse was

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:27.650
found out, King announced that Bachman had
died from “cancer of the pseudonym” and

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:32.450
wrote a book around him called The Dark Half.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:38.710
Then there are those who take pen names for
metatextual reasons, like Daniel Handler,

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:44.530
who is best known to readers around the world
as Lemony Snicket, the author of the A Series

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:49.990
of Unfortunate Events series. But the goofy
name wasn’t chosen just for purposes of

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:57.610
whimsy. Lemony Snicket is himself a character
and part of the narrative of his own stories,

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:06.650
and his elaborate, false identity is woven
in and out of the books just as much as the

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:08.860
actual protagonists.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.860
Then there are authors who take pen names
to try and reconcile with their own identities

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:17.680
from birth. The Bronte sisters, for example,
all wrote their only novels under matching

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:24.730
gender-ambiguous pseudonyms, with Charlotte
publishing Jane Eyre as Currer Bell, Emily

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:30.580
with Wuthering Heights as Ellis Bell, and
sweet forgotten Anne with The Tenant of Wildfell

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:38.990
Hall as Acton Bell! Said Charlotte on why
the Brontes decided to make pen names a family

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:48.580
affair:

00:07:48.580 --> 00:08:01.730
We had a vague impression that authoresses
are liable to be looked on with prejudice.

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:07.290
On the opposite side of this is poet and writer
Sapphire, author of Push. Unlike the Brontes,

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:12.620
Sapphire, born Ramona Lofton, picked a name
that not only embraced her identity as a Black

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:17.940
woman but also pushed back on stereotypes
of Black women, saying:

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:23.580
&nbsp;I took the name Sapphire at the height of
the New Age movement, when everybody was a

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:28.800
gemstone. At one time in African-American
culture, the name also had a very negative

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:34.469
connotation. Sapphire was, like, the evil,
razor-toting type of belligerent black woman,

00:08:34.469 --> 00:08:39.630
which was somehow attractive to me, especially
because my mother was just the opposite. And

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:44.740
I could picture the name on books; I couldn't
see "Push, by Ramona."

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:49.230
And then there are pen names that come from
a melange of ideas, like writer and activist

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:53.850
bell hooks, born Gloria Jean Watkins, who
said of her nom de plume:

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:58.581
When the feminist movement was at its zenith
in the late 60’s and early 70’s, there

00:08:58.581 --> 00:09:04.230
was a lot of moving away from the idea of
the person. It was: let’s talk about the

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:11.160
ideas behind the work, and the people matter
less. It was kind of a gimmicky thing, but

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:17.040
lots of feminist women were doing it. Many
of us took the names of our female ancestors—Bell

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:23.040
Hooks is my maternal great grandmother—to
honor them and debunk the notion that we were

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.060
these unique, exceptional women. We wanted
to say, actually, we were the products of

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:31.080
the women who’d gone before us.”&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:37.420
And sometimes—just sometimes—pen names
are straight politics. The Federalist Papers,

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:45.279
published between 1787and 1788 and written
to urge the ratification of the United States

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:51.270
Constitution, were all published under the
name of Publius. And if you remember

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:58.380
10th grade history or, heck, seen Hamilton,
you probably know that Publius is the pen

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:04.410
name of Alexander Hamilton, James Madison,
and John Jay.&nbsp;

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:09.880
The authors chose to do this not just for
the sake of anonymity itself, but in the name

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:15.140
of tradition at the time—many defenses for
and arguments against ratification were published

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:20.279
under pseudonyms, often taken from Roman antiquity
because those founding fathers just couldn’t

00:10:20.279 --> 00:10:29.310
help but simp the Romans. And Hamilton himself
very pointedly chose Publius as a nod to one

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:35.600
of the four aristocrats who led the overthrow
of the Roman monarchy.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:43.960
In her book examining the subject of pen names,
bluntly titled Nom de Plume, writer Carmela

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:45.260
Ciuraru muses:

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:52.860
“To a certain extent, all writing involves
impersonation—the act of summoning an authorial

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:58.870
“I” to create the speaker of a poem or
the characters in a novel…But some writers

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:04.730
are unable to engage in such alchemy, or don’t
want to, without relying on an alter ego.”

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:09.240
There is a lot of theoretical discussion on
how an author’s private life and choices

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:15.170
are intrinsically tied to the work they produce,
and how the two play off of each other in

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:20.740
the minds of readers, publishers, and those
who wish to biographize them. And so goes

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:27.210
the pen name, which—as we have seen, even
with this incredibly limited survey of authors—often

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:32.529
springs from deeply personal and unique reasons
respective to the author’s life.

00:11:32.529 --> 00:11:37.649
There are authors for whom a pen name felt
necessary and then went onto regret them,

00:11:37.649 --> 00:11:44.540
like Toni Morrison, born Chloe Wofford. Though
she took the name while studying it at Howard,

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:47.110
Morrison had this to say later in her life:

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:51.520
”People who call me Chloe are the people
who know me best,” she says. “Chloe writes

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:59.440
the books. Myself is kind of split. My name
is Chloe. And the rest is… that other person.

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:06.100
Who is able to feel, or pretends to feel,
or maybe really feels, or at least reacts

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:07.750
to celebrityhood.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:18.960
And then there are those like George Sand,
born Amantine Lucile Aurore Dupin, who became

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.291
one of the most popular writers in French
Romanticism under a male-sounding name, who

00:12:24.291 --> 00:12:31.220
also dressed in male attire, and who eventually
began to sign personal letters as George Sand.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:37.890
Said her contemporary, Victor Hugo: “George
Sand was an idea. She has a unique place in

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:42.300
our age. Others are great men ... she was
a great women.”&nbsp;

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:48.839
And then, sometimes, there is just no explaining
a pen name entirely. It’s taken as fact

00:12:48.839 --> 00:12:54.720
that one of the most famous American authors
and employer of a nom de plume, Mark Twain,

00:12:54.720 --> 00:13:03.470
had lifted his moniker from the riverboat
terminology for a measurement of “two fathoms”.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:08.700
And while the author has said as such in his
autobiography, scholarship doubts that claim

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:15.200
on the basis of … well, Twain himself, who
also said it was literally lifted from a dead

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:21.459
man—in this case, a steamboat captain named
Isaiah Sellers. To quote a letter written

00:13:21.459 --> 00:13:22.550
by Twain:

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:27.390
“Capt. Sellers used the signature, “Mark
Twain,” himself when he used to write up

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:31.620
the antiquities in the way of river reminiscences
for the New Orleans Picayune.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:37.791
He hated me for burlesquing them in an article
in the True Delta; so four years later, when

00:13:37.791 --> 00:13:45.420
he died, I robbed the corpse--that is, I confiscated
the nom de plume. I have published this vital

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:53.130
fact 3,600 times now. But no matter, it is
good practice; it is about the only fact that

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:56.399
I can tell the same way every time.

00:13:56.399 --> 00:14:01.890
The line between the author as a public personality
and the private person behind the pen is often

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:07.480
blurred, and a subject that raises many questions
and sometimes an unfair or dangerous degree

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:14.290
of the public trying to read between the lines.
For many authors, a pen name presents a solution—an

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:19.550
easy way to delineate between the story-teller’s
brand and the regular person tapping away

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:32.029
at a keyboard, trying to finish a manuscript,
and hoping someone sees the truth in it.&nbsp;

00:14:32.029 --> 00:14:36.690
